\pard\qc\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Abstract\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 Children are increasingly exposed to generative artificial intelligence (AI) systems during early development, yet little is known about how this affects selective trust when children interpret uncertain events. This study introduces a home-based event-boundary paradigm designed to test epistemic deference in a child-caregiver-AI triadic context. A 3-year-old child and caregiver viewed a 2:04 clip from Bluey with four predefined pauses: two event-boundary trials and two control trials. At each pause, the child completed three tasks: event segmentation ("same/new"), narrative prediction, and deference choice under caregiver-AI disagreement. Informant accuracy was counterbalanced by design (AI correct in boundaries; caregiver correct in controls; both 2/4 total), isolating contextual uncertainty from accumulated reliability. As a proof-of-concept pilot (N = 1), the study aimed to establish feasibility and refine mechanism-level hypotheses rather than estimate population effects. The protocol was fully executable and produced complete trial-level data. Overall robot choice was 3/4, but robot deference was lower at boundaries (0.50) than controls (1.00). Segmentation and prediction were perfect in the first half and null in the second half, suggesting attentional or fatigue effects. The pilot does not confirm the original directional hypothesis; instead, it supports a refined model in which deference reflects interactions among novelty bias, recent reliability, and subjective boundary detection. Findings justify a preregistered, adequately powered confirmatory study.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 Keywords: selective trust, early childhood, event boundaries, artificial intelligence, cognitive offloading, pilot study\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\qc\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Introduction\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 Young children calibrate trust by combining familiarity cues and informant reliability (Harris & Corriveau, 2011; Koenig & Harris, 2005). In current digital ecologies, children also encounter AI-generated predictions in daily contexts. This creates a developmental question: under uncertainty, does a child prioritize the familiar caregiver or an artificial informant?\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 Event-segmentation research indicates that narrative boundaries increase prediction error and model-updating demands (Zacks et al., 2007). These moments may function as periods of heightened epistemic openness. The present paradigm operationalizes this proposition by testing deference under controlled caregiver-AI disagreement at boundary versus control moments.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 A central design challenge is separating contextual uncertainty effects from simple reliability learning. If one informant is consistently more accurate, deference may reflect reinforcement history rather than boundary sensitivity. To address this, the paradigm counterbalances accuracy by condition: AI is correct at boundaries, caregiver is correct at controls, and both end with identical global accuracy (2/4 each).\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 The original hypothesis predicted higher AI deference at event boundaries. Because this is a proof-of-concept pilot, the primary goals were to evaluate operational feasibility, test measurement behavior, and refine mechanism-level hypotheses before confirmatory scaling.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\qc\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Method\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Design\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 This was a single-case proof-of-concept pilot using a within-session repeated-measures structure (4 trials: P1-P4). The inferential target was feasibility and mechanism discovery, not population-level confirmation.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Participant and Setting\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 One child (3 years old) participated with a caregiver in a domestic setting. The protocol was administered using a mobile device and physical response cards.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Materials and Stimulus\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 The stimulus was Bluey S1E1 ("Magic Xylophone"; 2:04). Four pause points were pre-specified:\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 1. P1 Boundary (~0:25), AI-correct trial.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 2. P2 Control (~0:50), caregiver-correct trial.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 3. P3 Boundary (~1:15), AI-correct trial.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 4. P4 Control (~1:35), caregiver-correct trial.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Procedure\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 At each pause, the same three-step protocol was administered:\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 1. Segmentation question ("same/new").\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 2. Two-option prediction.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 3. Deference choice under caregiver-AI disagreement.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 After each response, playback resumed for approximately 10-15 seconds so the child could observe the actual outcome.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Measures\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 Primary construct:\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 - Boundary deference effect: Delta_deference = P(robot | boundary) - P(robot | control)\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 Secondary outcomes:\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 - Segmentation accuracy by condition and session half.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 - Prediction accuracy by condition and session half.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 - Deference rate by condition.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 - Hesitation (yes/no) as a conflict indicator.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Analytic Strategy\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 Given N = 1 and four trials, analyses were descriptive:\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 1. Trial-level trajectory analysis.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 2. Condition-level proportions.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 3. First-half versus second-half temporal profile.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 4. Exact binomial 95% confidence intervals for proportions.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 No confirmatory population inferences were attempted.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\qc\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Results\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Feasibility and Data Capture\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 The protocol was completed (4/4 trials). All planned variables were captured without missing values. The counterbalanced accuracy manipulation was successfully implemented.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Trial-Level Pattern\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 Deference sequence: Robot (P1), Robot (P2), Caregiver (P3), Robot (P4). Hesitation occurred only in P1.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Condition-Level Outcomes\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 - Overall robot choice: 3/4 = 0.75, 95% CI [0.194, 0.994].\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 - Robot choice at boundaries: 1/2 = 0.50, 95% CI [0.013, 0.987].\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 - Robot choice at controls: 2/2 = 1.00, 95% CI [0.158, 1.000].\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 - Boundary deference effect (Delta_deference): -0.50.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Temporal Profile\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 - Segmentation accuracy, first half (P1-P2): 2/2 = 1.00, 95% CI [0.158, 1.000].\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 - Segmentation accuracy, second half (P3-P4): 0/2 = 0.00, 95% CI [0.000, 0.842].\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 - Prediction accuracy, first half: 2/2 = 1.00, 95% CI [0.158, 1.000].\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 - Prediction accuracy, second half: 0/2 = 0.00, 95% CI [0.000, 0.842].\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 The sharp first-half/second-half shift is compatible with attentional or fatigue contamination in a four-pause sequence for this age.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\qc\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Discussion\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 The pilot did not support the original directional prediction of higher AI deference at boundaries. However, it provided diagnostic evidence useful for theory refinement and design correction.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 Observed structure suggests a mixed mechanism:\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 1. Baseline novelty attraction toward the robot (3/4 choices).\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 2. Local caregiver selection after recent robot error (possible short-horizon reliability weighting).\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 3. Strong temporal degradation of segmentation/prediction (fatigue/disengagement).\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 4. Hesitation only at first boundary exposure (possible heightened epistemic conflict).\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 These patterns are more consistent with a model in which deference reflects interactions among novelty salience, recent reliability memory, and subjective boundary detection.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Validity Scope of an N = 1 Proof-of-Concept Pilot\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 This study supports procedural and diagnostic validity, not population validity.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 What is validly established:\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 1. Operational feasibility in natural home conditions.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 2. Trial-level measurement capture across all channels.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 3. Successful execution of counterbalanced informant-accuracy manipulation.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 4. Identification of major confounds that must be controlled before confirmatory inference.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 What is not claimed:\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 1. No population prevalence estimates.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 2. No confirmatory support for the original hypothesis.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 3. No stable effect-size estimation.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 This inferential boundary is methodologically necessary: proof-of-concept work tests whether the paradigm functions and which nuisance processes dominate, while confirmatory work estimates population effects under preregistered inference.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Limitations and Next Confirmatory Step\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 Key limitations were single-case scope, order/fatigue confounding, and binary hesitation coding without continuous latency. A confirmatory version should use:\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 1. Between-subjects structure with two pauses per child (one boundary, one control).\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 2. Brief robot familiarization before testing to reduce novelty inflation.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 3. Continuous latency coding plus deference choice.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 4. Preregistered hypotheses and analytic plan.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 5. Adequate sample size (projected near N ~ 130, approximately 65 per condition).\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
\pard\qc\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Conclusion\b0\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24 Within its explicit inferential boundaries, this N = 1 study is a scientifically valid proof-of-concept pilot. Its contribution is the validation of protocol mechanics and the refinement of a testable model for a powered confirmatory study.\par
\pard\ql\sl480\slmult1\f0\fs24\par
